      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "The objective of this study was to assess efficacy of a bed-assistive device during at-home recovery through self-reports of self-perceived recovery status and oral pain medication usage."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A case-control design was used to determine differences in self-perceived recovery measures and pain medication according to surgical procedure, device/no device, and age strata. Fifteen female patients undergoing abdominal hysterectomy (n = 6) or Cesarean-section (n = 9) procedures were recruited. Stratified sampling techniques were used to assign patients to the control (n = 8; age, 34.0 [6.3] years) and devices groups (n = 7; 40.7 [12.4] years). Both groups completed 12 questionnaires on perceived recovery status, activities of daily living, and pain medication usage over a five-week recovery period. Device group patients received training on device usage prior to surgery. Repeated measures ANOVA were used to analyse data and compare data across experimental groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In general, device users reported higher levels of energy, less pain interference, lower perceived pain, less reliance on pain medication and returned to activities of daily living faster than the control group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Self-assistive device usage provides significant subjective and objective improvements during at-home recovery and promotes patient independence."
        }
      ]},
